Title: Heterogeneous fiscal decentralisation in Italian regions
Abstract: This paper analyses Italy’s persistent asymmetric fiscal decentralisation by combining the OECD’s recent REGOFI and MUNIFI datasets with ISTAT and Eurostat data. The findings confirm major structural differences between special statute regions — with high fiscal autonomy and low dependence on transfers — and ordinary statute regions, which rely far more heavily on intergovernmental transfers. At the municipal level, however, the pattern reverses: municipalities in ordinary regions display higher fiscal autonomy than those in special statute regions. The study further documents a pronounced north–south divide, with southern territories exhibiting lower own-revenue capacity and higher vertical fiscal imbalances. These asymmetries have implications for cohesion, service provision and the risks posed by recent proposals for further differentiated autonomy. The results underscore the need for policy approaches that balance autonomy with equity, safeguarding service standards and territorial cohesion.

Classification-JEL: H71, H72, H77
Keywords: asymmetric decentralisation, fiscal autonomy, regional governments, municipal governments

